  "final_text": "All four steps are done in Kibana 9.5.5, and the rule reads back as enabled.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Flights' (id `d3d7af60-4c81-11e8-b3d7-01146121b73d`, index `kibana_sample_data_flights`) has 32 fields, including `timestamp` (date), `Carrier` (string), `Cancelled` (boolean), `DestCountry`, `FlightDelay`, and `DistanceKilometers`. The time field is `timestamp`.\n\n2. **Dashboard:** 'S4-d68da63c Flights' (id `8bddd06d-306f-42da-b85e-a3b30f0492f4`) has exactly two panels, both on the flights data view:\n   - \"Flights over time\": a line chart of count by date histogram on `timestamp`.\n   - \"Flights per carrier\": a pie chart of count grouped by terms on `Carrier` (top 10). I used a pie because the xy chart's x-axis rejects terms aggregations in this API version.\n\n3. **Alert rule:** 'S4-d68da63c Cancelled flights' (id `d6d155f5-906d-4424-b0f2-4d63bd7c55c4`) is an Elasticsearch query rule (`.es-query`). It runs every 1m and counts documents matching `Cancelled: true` over a 5m window, firing when the count is greater than 10. It was created with `enabled: true`, so I didn't need to enable it.\n\n4. **Rename:** I changed the carrier panel title to 'Flights by carrier' with a full PUT of the dashboard. The response shows both panels, with the time chart still titled 'Flights over time'. I then read the rule back and its `enabled` field is `true`.\n\nChoices I made: the rule uses the `alerts` consumer, following the spec's example, and an unfiltered `size` of 100. The request did not specify either.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
